    "explanation": "Page 36 of Topnotch Handout. The hardest\nlobe/segment to resect is segment 1/caudate\nlobe. The caudate lobe (posterior hepatic\nsegment I, Spigelian lobe) is situated upon the\npostero-superior surface of the liver on the right\nlobe of the liver, opposite the tenth and eleventh\nthoracic vertebrae. Caudate lobe resection is one\nof the most demanding procedures among\nhepatic resection, owing to its deep and complex\nlocation and its proximity to major vessels. The\ncaudate lobe is located beneath the major\nhepatic veins, between the portal vein and\nligamentum venosum, and anterior and partially\ncircumferential to the inferior vena cava (IVC).",
